46484375, 0.0187225341796875, -0.010986328125, 0.01580810546875, -0.0076141357421875, -0.00943756103515625, -0.0118408203125, -0.00859832763671875, -0.0196685791015625, 0.0006384849548339844, -0.00649261474609375, 0.0252838134765625, 0.01739501953125, -0.019012451171875, 0.004390716552734375, 0.01495361328125, -0.02337646484375, 0.01116943359375, 0.0047760009765625, 0.0118560791015625, 0.0189208984375, -0.003955841064453125, 0.0499267578125, -0.01120758056640625, 0.00222015380859375, 0.007274627685546875, 0.0247344970703125, -0.0147705078125, 0.0024166107177734375, 0.0292816162109375, -0.04638671875, -4.8100948333740234e-05, -0.01320648193359375, -0.016937255859375, 0.0005578994750976562, 0.006610870361328125, 0.010772705078125, -0.050933837890625, 0.0175323486328125, -0.0142822265625, 0.01085662841796875, -0.020538330078125, 0.006191253662109375, -0.007038116455078125, 0.00574493408203125], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "We investigate how the precise temporal order of excitatory and inhibitory synaptic inputs affects firing in sparse threshold-reset neural networks, particularly in the fast-synapse limit where synaptic delays vanish. While it is common to analyze such systems using aggregate synaptic measures, we show that the microscopic sequence of input arrivals can have a persistent macroscopic impact, even when standard convergence criteria are met.\n\nComponentwise weak convergence of signed synaptic kernels\u2014meaning that, for each neuron, the excitatory and inhibitory synaptic measures converge weakly (in distribution) to a limiting measure\u2014does not, by itself, determine the fast-synapse limit of a sparse threshold-reset network. Here, \"weak convergence\" refers to convergence of integrals against continuous test functions, and the \"Dirac delta at zero\" ($\\delta_0$) is the measure concentrated at zero delay, representing all input arriving instantaneously. Intuitively, this means that even if the total excitatory and inhibitory input to each neuron converges in a distributional sense, the precise order in which these inputs arrive can still matter for the neuron's response. \"Componentwise\" indicates that this convergence is checked separately for each neuron.\n\nTo illustrate this, we work within a causal event protocol with clamped refractoriness and smooth positive-delay kernels (each synaptic event has a strictly positive delay and the kernel is smooth). We construct two families of networks whose excitatory and inhibitory measures both converge weakly to $\\delta_0$, but with reversed microscopic arrival orders of excitation and inhibition. Specifically, in the \"excitatory-first\" family, excitation arrives just before inhibition, while in the \"inhibitory-first\" family, inhibition arrives first.\n\nThis arrival order is critical at the single-neuron level. In the excitatory-first family, a target neuron fires, while in the inhibitory-first family, it does not, precisely when the initial state $x$ and pulse masses $a$ (excitatory) and $b$ (inhibitory) satisfy $x+a-b<\\theta\\le x+a$, where $\\theta$ is the firing threshold. The \"strict margin\" condition\u2014meaning $x+a-\\theta>0$\u2014ensures that the neuron's post-excitation state exceeds threshold by a positive amount. This margin guarantees robustness: the firing outcome persists under small perturbations to the initial state, aggregate E/I pulse masses, or bounded drift in the system, because such changes cannot bridge the gap created by the margin.\n\nTo clarify how this microscopic effect scales up, we extend the analysis to the network level. Consider a moderately sparse random block graph\u2014a network divided into blocks (groups) of neurons, with each block size $q_N$ satisfying $q_N\\to\\infty$ and $q_N/N\\to0$ as the network size $N$ grows. The network is Dale-compatible, meaning each neuron is either purely excitatory or purely inhibitory, in accordance with Dale's law (a biological principle stating that a neuron releases only one type of neurotransmitter). Both network families share the same graph structure and initial data. Along any deterministic joint scaling $\\varepsilon_N\\downarrow0$, the difference in their population-averaged firing counts is $1/2+o_{L^1}(1)$, meaning that, on average, half the population exhibits a different firing response in the two systems, up to a vanishing error. (Here, $o_{L^1}(1)$ denotes a sequence of random variables converging to zero in $L^1$ norm, i.e., their expected absolute value goes to zero.) This demonstrates that the microscopic arrival order effect at the single-neuron level can aggregate to a macroscopic difference in network behavior, rather than being averaged out.\n\nA bounded-degree construction, together with a later probe (i.e., a subsequent input or test pulse delivered after the initial firing event), confirms that this discrepancy is macroscopic and can persist through the reset mechanism of the neurons. In this probe experiment, after the initial round of firing, a new input is delivered to all neurons. The probe reveals that neurons which fired in one family but not the other continue to respond differently to subsequent inputs, demonstrating that the difference in firing outcomes is not erased by the reset: the firing history induced by the initial arrival order persists and influences future responses.\n\nHowever, when synaptic kernels have fixed positive delays and only finitely many classes, the network can enter a stable regime. Before the firing threshold is grazed, typewise-mixing (i.e., sufficiently random mixing between neuron types, so that each neuron receives inputs from a representative sample of types) sparse networks converge to a delayed class mean-field system\u2014a simplified model where each neuron's input is replaced by the average over its type class\u2014where the effect of arrival order is averaged out. For directed Erd\u0151s-R\u00e9nyi graphs (random graphs where each possible connection is present independently with a fixed probability) with mean degree $\u03bb_N$ and empirical type distribution $\u03c0_N$, the deviation from the mean-field limit is bounded by $O_P(\u03bb_N^{-1/2}+\\|\u03c0_N-\u03c0\\|_1)$, where $O_P(\\cdot)$ denotes stochastic boundedness in probability (i.e., the probability that the deviation exceeds any fixed threshold goes to zero as $N$ increases), $\u03bb_N\\to\\infty$, $\u03bb_N/N\\to0$, and $\u03c0$ is the limiting type distribution. The intuition is that, with fixed delays and sufficient mixing, the precise order of input arrivals becomes irrelevant because the inputs are spread out in time and averaged over many sources. In contrast, in the singular collapse regime\u2014where the delay vanishes and all inputs arrive nearly simultaneously\u2014the arrival order can have a decisive effect, as the system cannot average out the microscopic differences.\n\nIn summary, our findings show that componentwise weak convergence of synaptic measures is insufficient to determine the fast-synapse limit in threshold-reset networks: the microscopic order of excitatory and inhibitory arrivals encodes information essential for the firing response, and this information can persist at the macroscopic level in sparse networks. This highlights the importance of considering arrival order in modeling and analyzing such systems, especially in the singular limit of vanishing delays. These results have potential implications for both biological and artificial neural systems, suggesting that precise spike timing and input order may play a functional role in network computation and memory. Limitations of this work include the focus on specific network architectures (e.g., block graphs and Dale-compatible structures) and event protocols. Future directions include investigating whether similar arrival-order effects arise in networks with more general connectivity patterns, under different dynamical rules (such as stochastic or continuous-time resets), or in the presence of synaptic plasticity. Open questions also remain regarding the impact of correlated input timings and the role of network heterogeneity in modulating these effects. Experimental or computational tests\u2014such as manipulating input timing in spiking neural network models or biological preparations\u2014could help assess the relevance of these findings in real-world systems."}}